    37	---
    38	
    39	## Definition
    40	
    41	Let $\kappa$ and $\lambda$ be cardinals ([[def-cardinal]]), and recall the
    42	notation of [[lem-cardinal-operations-are-well-defined]]:
    43	
    44	$$\kappa \sqcup \lambda = (\{0\} \times \kappa) \cup (\{1\} \times \lambda), \qquad {}^{\lambda}\kappa = \{\, h : h \text{ is a function } \lambda \to \kappa \,\}.$$
    45	
    46	**Sum and product.**
    47	
    48	$$\kappa \oplus \lambda \;:=\; \lvert \kappa \sqcup \lambda \rvert, \qquad \kappa \otimes \lambda \;:=\; \lvert \kappa \times \lambda \rvert .$$
    49	
    50	Both values exist in ZF and are cardinals: claim (c) of
    51	[[lem-cardinal-operations-are-well-defined]] well-orders each of the two sets
    52	explicitly, and [[lem-cardinality-of-a-well-orderable-set]] then supplies the
    53	least equinumerous ordinal. **No choice principle is used.**
    54	
    55	**Exponentiation.**
    56	
    57	$$\kappa^{\lambda} \;:=\; \lvert {}^{\lambda}\kappa \rvert ,$$
    58	
    59	the number of functions from a set of size $\lambda$ to a set of size $\kappa$.
    60	The right-hand side is defined exactly when ${}^{\lambda}\kappa$ is
    61	well-orderable. **Assuming the Axiom of Choice** ([[def-axiom-of-choice]]) every
    62	set is well-orderable ([[thm-well-ordering-theorem]]) and $\kappa^{\lambda}$ is
    63	defined for all cardinals; every statement on this page that writes
    64	$\kappa^{\lambda}$ for an infinite exponent says so in its own hypotheses.
    65	
    66	**Transport to arbitrary sets.** If $A$ and $B$ are well-orderable with
    67	$\lvert A \rvert = \kappa$ and $\lvert B \rvert = \lambda$, then
    68	
    69	$$\lvert A \sqcup B \rvert = \kappa \oplus \lambda, \qquad \lvert A \times B \rvert = \kappa \otimes \lambda, \qquad \lvert {}^{B}A \rvert = \kappa^{\lambda}$$
    70	
    71	whenever the sets on the left have cardinalities at all, because $A \approx \kappa$
    72	and $B \approx \lambda$ ([[def-equinumerous]]) and the three constructions
    73	respect $\approx$ (claim (a) of [[lem-cardinal-operations-are-well-defined]],
    74	[[def-injection-surjection-bijection]]). So the operations may be computed from
    75	any representatives.
    76	
    77	**Finite and infinite cardinals.** A cardinal $\kappa$ is **finite** when
    78	$\kappa \in \omega$ and **infinite** when $\omega \subseteq \kappa$, that is
    79	$\omega \le \kappa$; by trichotomy ([[lem-ordinal-trichotomy]]) and
    80	[[lem-omega-least-limit-ordinal]] every cardinal is exactly one of the two.
    81	
    82	## Remarks
    83	
    84	**The symbols $\oplus$ and $\otimes$ are not decoration.** Ordinal addition and
    85	ordinal multiplication ([[def-ordinal-addition]], [[def-ordinal-multiplication]])
    86	are defined on **the same objects** — cardinals are ordinals — and give
    87	**different values**. With $\omega$ read as a cardinal,
    88	$\omega \oplus \omega = \omega$, whereas the ordinal sum $\omega + \omega$ is
    89	strictly larger than $\omega$; and $\omega \otimes \omega = \omega$, whereas the
    90	ordinal product $\omega \cdot \omega$ is larger still. Writing both operations
    91	with $+$ and $\cdot$ would make every equation on this page ambiguous, so the
    92	cardinal operations get their own symbols and the plain $+$ and $\cdot$ on this
    93	page always mean the ordinal ones.
    94	
    95	**Exponentiation keeps the symbol, under a hard rule.** There is no comparably
    96	readable alternative to $\kappa^{\lambda}$, and
    97	[[rem-ordinal-versus-cardinal-exponentiation]] already records that
    98	$\alpha^{\beta}$ is used for two different operations: as ordinals
    99	$2^{\omega} = \omega$, while the cardinal $2^{\omega}$ counts the functions
   100	$\omega \to \{0,1\}$ and is uncountable. The rule adopted here, and followed on
   101	this page and its companion, is:
   102	
   103	> **In an exponential, the base and the exponent are always alephs, letters or
   104	> expressions denoting cardinals — $\kappa$, $\lambda$, $\mu$,
   105	> $\mathfrak{c}$, $\operatorname{cf}(\kappa)$, $\lvert A \rvert$ — or a natural
   106	> number read as a cardinal; never $\omega$, never $\omega_1$, and never a
   107	> letter denoting an ordinal, such as $\alpha, \beta, \gamma, \xi, \eta$.**
   108	
   109	So $2^{\aleph_0}$, $\kappa^{\lambda}$ and $\aleph_1^{\aleph_0}$ are cardinal
   110	exponentials, and an expression such as $\omega^{\omega}$ or $\alpha^{\beta}$ is
   111	never written here at all. Where a value has to be named in both readings, the
   112	two are given different letters.
   113	
   114	**What is being counted, in each case.** $\kappa \oplus \lambda$ is the size of
   115	two disjoint blocks laid side by side; the tagging in $\sqcup$ is what makes
   116	"disjoint" true even though one of $\kappa$ and $\lambda$ is always a subset of
   117	the other, so their intersection is the smaller of the two. $\kappa \otimes \lambda$
   118	is the size of a rectangle. $\kappa^{\lambda}$ is the number of ways to choose a
   119	value in $\kappa$ for each of $\lambda$ positions, independently. None of the
   120	three is sensitive to the order in which the elements are arranged, which is
   121	exactly what distinguishes them from the ordinal operations
   122	([[def-ordinal-exponentiation]] included), whose values depend on the arrangement.
   123	
   124	**The zero and one cases are not special.** $0 = \varnothing$ and $1 = \{0\}$ are
   125	cardinals ([[def-ordinal]]), and the definitions apply to them unchanged:
   126	$\kappa \sqcup 0 = \{0\} \times \kappa$, $\kappa \times 0 = \varnothing$, and
   127	${}^{0}\kappa = \{\varnothing\}$ has exactly one element, the empty function.
   128	The resulting unit laws are proved rather than stipulated, in
   129	[[lem-cardinal-arithmetic-basic-laws]].
